Yes, we can drastically reduce the loading time.
Right now, your application is fetching the data **sequentially**. It waits for Branches to finish downloading, then starts downloading Currencies, and then starts downloading CGLs.
If CGL takes 5 seconds, Branch takes 2 seconds, and Currency takes 1 second, your total load time is **8 seconds**.
To make this lightning fast, we can use Java's CompletableFuture to run all three database queries **in parallel** on separate CPU threads. This means the total load time will drop to just the time of the longest query (5 seconds instead of 8).
Here is the exact code to implement parallel loading in your ValidationMasterService.
### 1. The Parallel Loading Code
Update your refreshMasterData() method to look like this:
```java
    import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

    public void refreshMasterData() {
        log.info("MASTER_DATA_SYNC | Starting PARALLEL fetch from Oracle...");
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        try {
            // 1. Fire all three queries at the exact same time on background threads
            CompletableFuture<List<String>> branchFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 
                oracleJdbcTemplate.query("SELECT CODE FROM BRANCH_MASTER WHERE STATUS = 1", 
                (rs, rowNum) -> rs.getString(1))
            );

            CompletableFuture<List<String>> currencyFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 
                oracleJdbcTemplate.query("SELECT CURRENCY_CODE FROM CURRENCY_MASTER WHERE FLAG = 1", 
                (rs, rowNum) -> rs.getString(1))
            );

            CompletableFuture<List<String>> cglFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 
                oracleJdbcTemplate.query("SELECT CGL_NUMBER FROM CGL_MASTER WHERE STATUS = 1", 
                (rs, rowNum) -> rs.getString(1))
            );

            // 2. Wait for all of them to finish
            CompletableFuture.allOf(branchFuture, currencyFuture, cglFuture).join();

            // 3. Extract the data and perform the atomic nanosecond pointer swap
            this.activeBranches = new HashSet<>(branchFuture.get());
            this.activeCurrencies = new HashSet<>(currencyFuture.get());
            this.activeCgls = new HashSet<>(cglFuture.get());

            long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
            log.info("MASTER_DATA_SYNC_SUCCESS | Loaded in {} ms. CGLs: {} | Branches: {} | Currencies: {}", 
                    duration, this.activeCgls.size(), this.activeBranches.size(), this.activeCurrencies.size());

        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("MASTER_DATA_SYNC_FAIL | Retaining old RAM cache. Error: {}", e.getMessage());
        }
    }

```
### 2. Database Level (Covering Indexes)
To make the actual Oracle execution instant, you must ensure you have **Covering Indexes** on those tables. If you only have a standard index, Oracle still has to read the table data. A covering index puts the specific data you requested directly into the index tree, meaning Oracle never even touches the actual table.
If you have access to run SQL on your Oracle database, ask your DBA to apply these exact indexes. It will force Oracle to use an "Index Fast Full Scan," which takes milliseconds.
```sql
-- Creates covering indexes so Oracle only reads the B-Tree, bypassing the table completely
CREATE INDEX IDX_BRANCH_STATUS_CODE ON BRANCH_MASTER (STATUS, CODE);
CREATE INDEX IDX_CURRENCY_FLAG_CODE ON CURRENCY_MASTER (FLAG, CURRENCY_CODE);
CREATE INDEX IDX_CGL_STATUS_NUMBER ON CGL_MASTER (STATUS, CGL_NUMBER);

```
### 3. Increase Fetch Size
In your provided code, you were using ps.setFetchSize(2000). If you are pulling over 50,000 CGLs, that is still 25 network round trips.
Because we are only fetching a single String column (which takes almost zero memory), you can safely increase the fetch size for this specific query to **10,000**. This will pull 10,000 rows in a single network packet, drastically cutting down the network latency between your server and Oracle.
Combine these three techniques (Parallel Threads + Covering Indexes + Larger Fetch Size), and your master data will reload in the blink of an eye.
